### How the responsive behavior works

| Viewport | Columns | Rule |
|---|---|---|
| < 600px | 1 | Base `grid-template-columns: 1fr` |
| 600–899px | 2 | `@media (min-width: 600px)` |
| ≥ 900px | 3 | `@media (min-width: 900px)` |

### Alternative: auto-fit (no media queries)

If you'd rather let the browser decide the column count based on available space:

```css
.product-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 280px), 1fr));
  gap: 1.25rem;
}
```

The `min(100%, 280px)` is important — it prevents the track from overflowing on narrow screens. This gives 1 column below ~280px, 2 in the mid-range, and 3+ on wide screens.

### A few other tips
- **`aspect-ratio` on the media wrapper** keeps every card the same height regardless of image dimensions — no layout shift.
- **`margin-top: auto` on the price** pushes the price and button to the bottom of each card, so buttons align across a row.
- **`loading="lazy"`** on images speeds up initial load for long grids.
- **Container queries** (if you need the grid to respond to its parent rather than the viewport) work great here too:

```css
.card-wrapper { container-type: inline-size; }

@container (min-width: 600px) {
  .product-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
}
@container (min-width: 900px) {
  .product-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
}
```

