## Key points

**Mobile-first breakpoints.** The base rule is one column; columns are *added* as the viewport grows. This avoids the classic bug where a desktop-first `min-width` rule leaves mobile users with a cramped 3-column layout.

**`aspect-ratio` + `object-fit: cover`** keeps every image the same height without fixed pixel dimensions, so cards stay aligned no matter the source image's proportions.

**`flex: 1` on `.card__body` and `margin-top: auto` on `.card__footer`** make the price/button row align to the bottom of every card, even when descriptions differ in length.

**`loading="lazy"`** on images keeps the initial page load fast for long product lists.

## Alternative: fully fluid grid (no media queries)

If you'd rather the layout adapt continuously instead of snapping at fixed breakpoints, replace the grid rules with:

```css
.product-grid {
  display: grid;
  gap: var(--gap);
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 22rem), 1fr));
}
```

The `min(100%, 22rem)` guard is important — without it, a card's minimum width can exceed a narrow phone's width and cause horizontal overflow. At a 1200px container this yields 3 columns; the count changes automatically as the container resizes.

## Optional: splitting into separate files

For a real project, move the `<style>` block into `styles.css` and link it in the head:

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
```

And add `loading="lazy"`, `width`/`height` attributes on images (as above) to prevent layout shift while images load.

